course: dictionary
lesson: 9533
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word stitch is a good example. Read the sentences around it first, then ask which meaning fits.
explain: «A teenager in Wales cut a bagel while holding it in her palm; the knife slipped straight into her hand and she needed stitches.» Here stitches are not sewing. The cut was deep, so a doctor closes it with stitches so it can heal.
explain: «A boy in Bristol skipped warming up and his helmet at the skate park; he fell, hit his head and needed stitches above his eye.» Stitches still mean medical stitches here. If the text talks about a needle or thread, the meaning changes back to sewing.
explain: Try the swap check: put your meaning in place of the word. If «a doctor closed the cut with threads» still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: A stitch is one loop of thread made with a needle when sewing, or one loop of wool in knitting.
fact: Stitches can also be the threads a doctor uses to close a deep cut so it can heal.
fact: A stitch can be a sudden sharp pain in your side when you run; slowing down and breathing deeply helps.
fact: To stitch means to sew, and in design and technology children learn running stitch and cross-stitch.
fact: The phrase «in stitches» means laughing a lot, so a phrase can mean something very different from its single words.
exercise: «A family dog in Ohio had been gentle for years; when a toddler grabbed its tail it bit her face and she needed stitches.» What does stitches mean here? | Loops of wool in knitting | A sharp pain in the side | Loops of thread for sewing | *Threads a doctor uses to close a deep cut
exercise: In PE, Maya gets a stitch while running. What does stitch mean? | To sew a cloth together | A loop of thread on a needle | A loop of wool | *A sudden sharp pain in your side
exercise: Find and copy the word that means to sew: «In design and technology, we will stitch a bag from felt.» | design | *stitch | bag | felt
test: Grandma knitted a jumper and counted every stitch. What is a stitch here? | A cut closed by a doctor | *One loop of wool made in knitting | To sew a button on | A pain when running
test: «The children were in stitches at the funny film.» What does «in stitches» mean? | Sewing very neatly | Crying quietly | Hurt in the side | *Laughing a lot
exam: «A scientist in 1799 looked for stitches on the platypus skin, sure that a joker had sewn on a duck's bill.» What does stitches mean here? | Sharp pains from running | Loops of wool from knitting | Medical threads that close a cut | *Loops of thread made with a needle when sewing
